<section image-layout="blank" id="54Yssyw4l1"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="Vp3q-YxvKg" vertical-align="center" colspan="34"><labels><label variant="outline">Log entry 01</label></labels><h2>The log begins hot and dense</h2><p font-size="18pt">The observable universe was once in an extremely hot, dense state. As space expanded, the universe cooled. Measurements place this history at about <b>13.8 billion years</b>.</p><p font-size="18pt">This is not a bomb’s launch time in a pre-existing void. The model describes the universe changing with time—not matter flying into an outer sea.</p><p role="caption">Source: <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/overview/">NASA · Universe Overview</a></p></grid-item><grid-item id="926XgcszlN" padding="0" colspan="26"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/gvVGNyol0cPkAkVlmuh1A.png" saved-media-id="5iucvfr32zpvpk8" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Copperplate scientific concept illustration of the uniformly hot dense early cosmos extending beyond every edge of the frame, nested unlabeled coordinate meshes and diffused cyan radiation traces with restrained oxidized-brass instrument arcs, abyssal blue field, no center, no outward blast, absolutely no text, numbers, symbols, tick labels, or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1440,&quot;height&quot;:1440}"><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="g8p68et-kQ" background-color="surface" padding="10pt 14pt"><p role="caption"><b>Concept illustration</b> · not observational data or a map of a blast.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>